- op: discuss  # 67af
  at: '2026-07-31T11:10:05Z'  # 67af
  by: agent  # 67af
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 67af
  clock: 3  # 67af
  nonce: 67af  # 67af
  message: "CLOSING as done. PR #122, merged, dev 9f0a163833.\n\nBEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE — this item's own close condition asked for the end-to-end repro rather than a unit test, and that is what was run:\n\n  contains_out per container       0 -> 3   (scala, cpp, kotlin)\n  slice --entry Color --reverse    1 -> 4   (scala, cpp, kotlin)\n\nVerified on a generated multi-language corpus in /tmp with a fresh cache, after the analyzer tests were already green — because the whole lesson of WI-duguk is that a green isolation test is not evidence of live expansion.\n\nWHAT WAS EMITTED, per this analyzer's own naming convention, since the containment linker splits on the first of `#`, `::`, `.` it finds:\n  scala   `Color.Red`   (matches its f\"{owner}.{prop_name}\" fields)\n  cpp     `Color::Red`  (matches its f\"{owner_name}::{field_name}\" fields)\n  kotlin  `Color.RED`   (matches its f\"{enclosing_class}.{prop_name}\" fields)\n\nKOTLIN CONFIRMED AS FILED, and it shaped the fix: `enum class` is emitted as kind=\"class\" with an \"enum\" modifier, and there is no kind=\"enum\" anywhere in kotlin.py. The member loop therefore gates STRUCTURALLY on the presence of an `enum_class_body` node rather than on the kind or the modifier string. `class` is in both containment.CONTAINER_KINDS and the slicer's set, so members root and expand with no kind change needed — but this is why the verification above is a containment count and not an enum-kind query, and why the separate question of whether Kotlin should emit kind=\"enum\" was NOT bundled here.\n\nTWO TRAPS THAT WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A PARTIAL FIX, both found by dumping the live grammar rather than assuming the shape matched an already-drained language:\n  * scala has TWO member node types — `simple_enum_case` (`case Red`) and `full_enum_case` (`case Node(v: Int)`) — and `case Green, Blue` is ONE `enum_case_definitions` holding TWO siblings. A per-group loop drops every case after a comma while leaving the container looking populated, which is the exact shape that made the java/swift enum gap invisible for so long.\n  * cpp's emission sits INSIDE the `if name_node:` guard. An anonymous `typedef enum { P, Q } Anon;` emits no container at all, so emitting its members would produce symbols whose dotted owner does not exist — orphans by construction, strictly worse than the recall miss.\n\nONE EXISTING TEST CHANGED, and it was PINNING the defect rather than merely tolerating it: `test_scala_field_variable_symbols.py::test_enum_body_val_emits_field` asserted `_fields(syms) == {\"Color.rgb\"}` over a fixture that has always contained `case Red`. The exact-set assertion encoded the missing case as the EXPECTED answer, so anyone fixing scala enum members would have seen it go red and had to decide whether their own fix was wrong. A `>=` or membership assertion would have passed either way and said nothing. Worth noting as a companion to the test-selection defect class: a test that cannot be selected is indistinguishable from one that passes, and a test that pins the bug is worse than one that is missing.\n\nSTANDING ENFORCEMENT: a dedicated test file, `test_scala_cpp_kotlin_enum_members.py`, 15 tests. NOT a matrix cell — the G2 emission-parity matrix gates eight core-availability fixture languages and its module docstring names scala/kotlin/cpp as ungated, citing this item. That disclosure is now accurate in a stronger sense: the languages remain ungated by the matrix, but the defect it could not see is fixed and separately tested.\n\nCOST: mypy delta +0. Pure recall improvement, no type-surface cost. 6886 tests pass, scoped coverage clean.\n\nNOT ADDRESSED, and left open deliberately (adjacent, found while reading scala.py): `_get_enclosing_type` matches only class/object/trait definitions, so a `def` inside an enum body is emitted as kind=\"function\" with a BARE name (`label`, not `Color.label`) and does not root either. Adding \"enum_definition\" there would fix it, but it changes existing symbol names and kinds for anyone already consuming them, so it is a separable decision rather than part of this fix."  # 67af
